9. Examples
---

Time to put everything together. Here is a full user journey of a user called "foo", who wants to run a long running job on a busy cluster:

```
# SSH into HDN
ssh foo@wpia-lxhn.hpc.dide.ic.ac.uk


# Check disk usage, don't want to get too close
# to 2TB
du -hs ~/
> 1.8T	/mnt/gpu-storage/homes/foo/


# Time to clean up
rm -r old_checkpoints_safe_to_delete


# Check disk usage again
du -hs ~/
> 200M	/mnt/gpu-storage/homes/foo/

# Good to go!


# Make folder and script
# In practice you would probably use `git` to pull
# changes or copy files over with `scp`, `vi` is
# just a text editor
mkdir project_final_final_v2
cd project_final_final_v2
vi script.sh
chmod +x script.sh


# Show script
cat script.sh
> #!/bin/bash
> #SBATCH --gres=gpu:nvidia_l40s:2
>
> echo $CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES
> analyse    # Kowalski, Analysis


# Which nodes have nvidia_l40s?
sinfo -o "%10N  %4c  %10m  %35G"


# Which ones are free?
sinfo
squeue

# Sadly all are busy. I have to run this script
# 4 times, needing a total of 8 GPUs. I should
# probably send them to one node if the cluster
# is very busy, okay will send them to gpu4


# Ready to submit the job but need to check my
# ticket
klist


# Refresh ticket
kinit


# Submit the jobs
sbatch --nodelist=gpu4 ./script.sh
> sbatch: Submitted batch job 1680
sbatch --nodelist=gpu4 ./script.sh
> sbatch: Submitted batch job 1681
sbatch --nodelist=gpu4 ./script.sh
> sbatch: Submitted batch job 1682
sbatch --nodelist=gpu4 ./script.sh
> sbatch: Submitted batch job 1683
sbatch --nodelist=gpu4 ./script.sh
> sbatch: Submitted batch job 1684


# That last one was a mistake!
scancel 1684


# Set a reminder on phone for a week from now
# to check back on the job and enjoy other
# healthier hobbies in life
exit


# Time passes... one week later
ssh foo@wpia-lxhn.hpc.dide.ic.ac.uk


# Where is my job in the queue?
squeue

# Still in the queue


# Do I need to refresh my ticket?
auks -g
klist


# Probably should refresh my ticket
kinit
klist
auks -a


# Touch some grass, enjoy hobbies...
exit


# Time passes... one week later
ssh foo@wpia-lxhn.hpc.dide.ic.ac.uk
squeue

# Two jobs have been picked up with two jobs
# still in queue, I may need to refresh my
# ticket again if needed


# Might have too many hobbies...
exit


# Time passes... couple days later
ssh foo@wpia-lxhn.hpc.dide.ic.ac.uk
squeue

# Jobs have finished


# View output
cat slurm-1680.out
```


Here is a summarised journey for a user who is unsure about their GPU utilisation and wants to monitor GPU usage on a smaller job
```
ssh foo@wpia-lxhn.hpc.dide.ic.ac.uk

mkdir smaller_job
cd smaller_job
vi test.sh
cat test.sh
> #!/bin/bash
> #SBATCH --gres=gpu:nvidia_l40s:2
>
> echo $CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES
> smaller-analyse    # kowalski, analysis

kinit
sbatch --nodelist=gpu6 test.sh
> sbatch: Submitted batch job 8142

# Just after job has been picked up
cat slurm-8142.out
> 2,3

# Job was allocated GPUs 2 and 3 on gpu6

# Run the following command a couple of times
# noting the GPU power draw, memory allocated
# and utilisation
srun --nodelist=gpu6 nvidia-smi

# If they are not high, investigate why your
# code isn't using resources efficiently
```


Here is a summarised journey for a user, foo, who is blocked by some users, bar and baz, and wants to know their jobs' time limits and GPU utilisations on gpu5. This has slightly more advanced commands that you do not need to know but useful if you want to check someone else's utilisation
```
ssh foo@wpia-lxhn.hpc.dide.ic.ac.uk
squeue


# Can see time limit
scontrol show jobs 123


# nvidia-smi to find processes running on GPUs
# and GPU utilisation
srun --nodelist=gpu5 nvidia-smi

# One of the processes consistently has pretty
# low GPU utilisation


# Can make a note of the process id and run
# command below to get user that owns this
# process (first column)
srun --nodelist=gpu5 ps -ef | grep <pid>

# The first column is their DIDE username but
# to find their full name so you can contact
# them on teams you can run
getent passwd <username>
```
